// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row bc_patmovebind_at_ref_binding_twin (tier 3). `ref b @ E::B`
// under a `match *r` BINDS BY REFERENCE and moves nothing; logosc answers
// "cannot move out of 'r', which is behind a reference". rustc 1.98.1
// --edition 2024 ACCEPTS the same program (measured 2026-09-22: the twin
// compiles with no diagnostic).
//
// THE MODE IS NOT IN THE AST. `PatAt` carries no mode field at all, so
// `ref b @ …` and `b @ …` are the SAME NODE — sema_stmt.cpp says so at its
// `case ps::Code::At` — and the borrow checker cannot distinguish them. Reading
// the binding's TYPE instead was tried and does not work: it is `E`, not `&E`.
// The repair is a mode flag on `PatAt`, threaded grammar -> schema -> mirror ->
// view, which is why this is a row and not a patch.
// PAIR 4 of 6, CASUALTY GUARD — `ref b @ E::B` is LEGAL and the first correct
// spelling of arm 4 refused it (MEASURED rc=1). `PatAt` carries
// {name, sub, type, bind_slot} and NO mode field at all, so `ref b @ E::B` and
// `b @ E::B` are indistinguishable at that node; the arm therefore makes no
// claim about its own binding and walks only the SUB-pattern, whose node may
// carry a mode. The cost of that silence is that `b @ E::B` by value is missed
// — a HOLE, which is the right way round: a missing refusal is a row, a wrong
// refusal is a legal program killed.
package bc_patmovebind_at_ref_binding_twin;
struct P { n: i64 }
impl Drop for P { fn drop(self: &mut P) {} }
enum E { A(P), B }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let e = E::A(P { n: 1i64 });
    let r: &E = &e;
    match *r {
        E::A(ref d) => {
            let v: i64 = d.n;
            return (v - 1i64) as i32;
        }
        ref b @ E::B => { return 1i32; }
    }
}
